You are doing a READ-ONLY orphan-interface scan of the C++ part of the repository at /home/ubuntu/个人学习/AI-powered-Network-Diagnostics (do NOT modify any file, do NOT run builds that write into the repo).

Scope: `server/src/` (including `server/src/bpf/`), `server/include/`, `client/`. Ignore `build-arm64/`, `Large-Model-Application/`.

Definition of orphan (must be evidence-based): a production surface with no real consumer. Candidates:
1. internal API / class / method declared in a header for cross-file use whose only caller is under `server/test/` (a test-only caller is NOT a production consumer);
2. D-Bus methods / signals / interface members exposed by `server/src/dbus_service.cpp` that no in-repo client calls (check `client/client.cpp`, `client/weaknet_client.h`, `client/weaknet_cli.cpp`) and no documented external consumer exists;
3. configuration keys in `config.yaml` / `server/src/weaknet_config.cpp` / `server/include/weaknet_config.hpp` that are parsed but never consulted by a live code path (parsed-never-read);
4. eBPF programs in `server/src/*.bpf.c` and `server/src/bpf/*.bpf.c` that are compiled but never loaded/attached, or loaded but their map output is never read;
5. callback/plugin registrations that are registered but never dispatched, or dispatched but never registered (look at `monitor_plugin.hpp`, `monitor_manager.cpp`, `monitor_plugins.cpp`, `monitor_ebpf_plugins.cpp`, `assurance/action_registry.*`);
6. build targets in CMakeLists.txt that nothing depends on and nothing installs.

Known prior context (do not re-report as new unless you can prove it regressed): the project has already removed unsubscribed D-Bus signals (see openspec/tasks/2026-10-19-stop-unsubscribed-dbus-signals.md), wired orphan cloud surfaces (2026-10-19-cloud-orphan-surfaces-wiring-and-cleanup.md), purged dead code (2026-10-19-edge-cloud-hardening-and-dead-code-purge.md), and added a tcp-stats consumer (2026-10-19-tcp-stats-consumer-and-k4.md). Read those task files if useful to avoid duplicate reports.

Method: for each candidate, be precise. Use ripgrep for symbol names across production paths only (exclude server/test to prove absence of production caller). Report ONLY candidates where you have verified evidence: exact `file:line`, the search you ran and the scope, and the classification (`no-caller`, `test-only-caller`, `dead-dispatch`, `parsed-never-read`, `superseded-entrypoint`, `unreachable-artifact`). Do not pad with weak findings; an empty result is acceptable. Note build warnings you observe in source (e.g. unused functions) only if they indicate genuinely dead production code.

Output format: a markdown list, most severe first. For each: title, `file:line`, evidence (paste the actual grep counts / symbol names you searched), classification, concrete consequence, and one recommended disposition (remove / replan with a real consumer / document as intentional incubation with exit condition). Also list any candidates you investigated and REJECTED with the reason, in a short second list, so I can trust the coverage. Keep it under 900 words.

